"""Deviations D14 onward (22 to 23 October 2026), inserted after D13 in Section 12."""
D = [
"D14. Timing of the addendum (22 October 2026). The Section 14 plans were written after the Study 1 results in Section 13 were known, though before the data each plan uses were examined. The person fixing them knew that the Study 1 content effects were null. This does not bias the new tests toward any particular result, but they are not independent of the first run in the way a preregistered confirmatory study would be.",
"D15. Dictionary file. The MAC-D files used come from the eMACDscore package (github.com/medianeuroscience/eMACDscore). The vice file matches the size of the OSF original (osf.io/86qry). The virtue file is 28,332 bytes, 9 bytes smaller than the OSF listing. The OSF file could not be downloaded from this environment, so the difference (probably one short entry) is unidentified. Every CCI score in Sections 13 and 15 uses the package file.",
"D16. Study 2 smallest effect of interest. The bound of 0.10 SD per SD of CCI (Section 14.1) was set without calibration. The positive control, the published trickster-punishment measure, is about 0.03 SD per SD in the immigrant sample. So an equivalence result at 0.10 SD rules out only effects about three times the size of the strongest known folklore effect. In Section 15, any immigrant effect below 0.10 SD is reported as small, not as absent.",
"D17. Study 2 measurement. Motif descriptions are one or two sentences long, and only 26 percent of motifs contain a MAC-D virtue term. Deference terms appear in 0.35 percent of motifs and fairness terms in 0.08 percent, so the z-scores of these two domains rest on a handful of motifs, and the folklore CCI mostly reflects family and heroism words. The index was kept as specified.",
"D18. H8-3 covariate. The plan adjusted crisis severity and ruler removal for population and era, but the first script (h8_improved.py) included only era. A corrected model with log population (h8_3_population.py, complete cases) is reported alongside it in Section 15.",
"D19. H4b detail fixed in code. Section 14.2 did not specify four rules: which tradition counts when coders correct the database; how unknown conqueror writing is handled; the minimum number of events; and the number of permutation draws. These were fixed in the header of h4b_analysis.py before the adjudicated codes were read. Coders worked under a shared web-search budget of 8 searches per batch of 10 episodes.",
"D20. H9a sample and texts (23 October 2026). Full public-domain English ethnographies published before 1929 are unevenly available. The 46 verified candidates covered 36 top-level families: 21 were in the Americas, and none was found for Uralic, Turkic, Tungusic or Caucasian peoples. The fixed rule took one society per family at random, then up to 6 per region with a random fill. It gave 30 societies: 13 in the Americas, 6 in Asia, 5 in Oceania, 4 in Africa and 2 in Europe and northern Eurasia. Several texts are topical rather than general accounts: a calendar history (Kiowa), a mythology (Tsimshian), a study of religion (Khoikhoi), a study of magic (Orokaiva), a language-and-folklore volume (Nandi) and a multi-tribe survey (Natchez). Two are not fieldwork monographs: a missionary's account (Mongols) and a social study (Russian peasants). One predates academic ethnography (Lane on Cairo, 1836). The Shan were judged distinct from the excluded Central Thai. Monographs bound in larger volumes were cut at their headings, with no regard to content; the Mailu paper, for example, was taken from its journal volume. Passages were extracted by the fixed rule: 2,368 items, with up to 12 per behaviour per society, and fewer where a text had fewer matching paragraphs (fairness passages were scarce).",
"D22. Errors found by the independent verification (23 October 2026). First, the MAC-D scorer keyed dictionary entries on whitespace only. Because text tokens never contain hyphens, the 39 hyphenated entries (for example 'father-in-law', 'give-and-take', 'even-handed') could never match. Corrected, the tradition CCIs correlate 0.9999 with the originals (largest change 0.009). Greek, Norse-Germanic and Mesopotamian, which were nearly tied, swap ranks 4 to 6; the Study 1 results are unaffected in substance. In Study 2 the bug changes 4 motif flags, and the political-scale estimate moves from +0.077 to between +0.071 and +0.076. Second, Study 2 dropped 8 motifs whose incidence columns end in an underscore (effect below 0.001). Third, the first Study 2 summary reported the immigrant model without the individual controls the plan specified. The planned model gives +0.015 SD (90 percent interval -0.013 to 0.043) for the CCI, +0.036 (p = 0.002) for the net index, and b = 1.07 (p = 0.008) for the trickster control; Section 15 reports these. The stemming of -eth and -est endings, which the verifier flagged, is specified in the corpus rules and was kept.",
"D23. Revised H8 test (23 October 2026, at the author's request). Seshat's moralizing enforcement of rulers measures what supporters claimed about rulers, not what rulers did. H8 is therefore re-tested on conduct, using expert-coded V-Dem data for autocracies from 1789 to 2025 (Section 14.5). The plan was fixed in h8r_plan.md before the models were run. After freezing, two bugs were fixed (h8r_changes_after_freeze.diff). Columns named C and D clashed with the formula syntax and were renamed. Models were refitted on complete cases so that absent fixed-effect levels do not produce empty columns and undefined standard errors. Neither fix changes any estimate. The original Seshat-based H8 results stay in Sections 13 and 15 as the test of the original exposure.",
"D21. H8 follow-up (23 October 2026). The tests of whether ruler removal and internal collapse are costly to the society were added on the author's request, after the H8 results were known. They are not in Section 14. Their plan was fixed in h8_followup_plan.md before the outcomes were computed. Two checks were added after seeing the results and are labelled as such: the survivor-only model, and complexity built only from non-scale measures. The earlier suggestion in this run that deposition might reflect accountability is withdrawn, because the follow-up does not support it.",
]

ADD145 = ("14.5 H8 revised: ruler conduct and long-term outcomes (fixed 23 October 2026, before the models were run)",
"Exposure: what rulers do, measured in V-Dem v15 (expert-coded, 1789 to 2025) for autocratic country-years. Dominance is the mean of political killings, torture and civil society repression. Cooperation is the mean of executive respect for the constitution, judicial and legislative constraints, and public-goods spending. Strategy is cooperation minus dominance, standardized. (A) Religious legitimation (1900 to 2023): association with religious freedom, repression, constraints, public goods and personalist legitimation, with region or country fixed effects, compared with nationalist and socialist legitimation. (B1) Forward outcomes from each autocracy-year: growth of GDP per head over 10 and 20 years, change in life expectancy over 20 years, civil war onset and coups within 10 years. Controls: income, population, decade and region, with errors clustered by country. (B2) For each autocratic regime: whether it ended violently from within (coup, assassination, civil war defeat, uprising) rather than in an orderly way. (B3) Regime duration. Supported if strategy predicts better outcomes in all of B1 and fewer violent ends in B2. The full plan is in h8r_plan.md.")
